    <DataNode elementId="ANDROST4ENE" textLabel="androstenedione" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="AEMFNILZOJDQLW-QAGGRKNESA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="680" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: ANDROST4ENE &quot;Androst-4-ene-3,17-dione&quot;| (androstenedione) is a key intermediate of microbial steroid metabolism. It is a naturally occurring steroid hormone produced in the gonad or ovary, and an immediate precursor of |FRAME: TESTOSTERONE| in males and |FRAME: CPD-352| and |FRAME: ESTRONE| in females |CITS: [18329874]|. The compound was first isolated in 1931 from a male policeman&apos;s urine and named androsterone (andro = male, ster = sterol, one = ketone) |CITS: [Butenandt34]|. The chemical structure of androsterone was reported soon after |CITS: [Ruzicka34]|.</Comment>
